import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
---

<HelpLayout title="Exercises" description="How to use the A-Z exercise library, search and filter exercises, and create custom exercises in Badger.">
  <h1>Exercises</h1>

  <p>Badger comes with around 370 exercises in 8 categories. Use them as they are, edit them, add your own exercises, or add categories of your own.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="library">Exercise library</h2>
  <p>Open <strong>More → Exercise library</strong> to see every exercise you have in one alphabetical list.</p>
  <p>Each row shows an equipment glyph, the exercise name, and its type and category underneath. A star means it's a favorite. Tap a row to open the exercise's page.</p>
  <p>Letter headings divide the list. Tap a letter in the strip down the right edge to jump to it, or drag your thumb along the strip to scrub through.</p>

  <h2 id="search-filter">Search and filter</h2>
  <p>Type in the search box at the top to narrow the list by name as you type.</p>

  <h3>Filter chips</h3>
  <p>Under the search box are the chips <strong>All</strong>, <strong>Favorites</strong>, and one per category. Tap <strong>Favorites</strong> for only your starred exercises, or a category chip for only that category.</p>

  <h3>Muscle filter</h3>
  <p>Tap the <strong>muscle icon</strong> in the top bar. A body diagram sits above a scrollable list:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tap a region on the diagram to filter by that muscle group.</li>
    <li>Tap a group header in the list to select the whole group.</li>
    <li>Tap a single muscle to narrow to just that muscle.</li>
    <li>The active filter shows as a pill below the search box. Tap it to clear.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Equipment filter</h3>
  <p>Tap the <strong>equipment icon</strong> in the top bar to filter by barbell, dumbbell, cable, machine, bodyweight, kettlebell, band or other. The active filter shows as a pill below the search box.</p>
  <p>An exercise with no equipment type shows an empty-bracket glyph on its row. Open the exercise and set the type to fix it.</p>
  <p>Search, chips, muscle filter and equipment filter all combine. Type "row", pick the Back chip and filter to cable, and you see only what matches all three.</p>

  <h2 id="favorites">Favorites</h2>
  <p>Open the <strong>⋮</strong> menu on an exercise's row and tap <strong>Favorite</strong>. A star appears on the row, and the <strong>Favorites</strong> chip shows only your starred exercises. Tap <strong>Unfavorite</strong> in the same menu to remove it.</p>

  <h2>Avoided exercises</h2>
  <p>Mark an exercise you want to stay away from (an injury, a preference, equipment you don't have): open the <strong>⋮</strong> menu on its row and tap <strong>Mark as avoided</strong>.</p>
  <p>In the library it gets an "Avoided" tag and a dimmed name, and you can still log it. It's left out of <a href="/help/routines/#plan-b">Plan B</a> suggestions, and <a href="/help/settings/#ai-context">Copy context for AI</a> lists it so an AI knows to leave it out. Tap <strong>Remove from avoided</strong> in the same menu to clear it.</p>

  <h2 id="detail">The exercise page</h2>
  <p>An exercise's page leads with your history for it: every session you've logged, newest first.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The record key</strong> at the top, beside the illustration, shows your best set and the estimated one-rep max it works out to. Tap it for the full progress screen, with the graph and the rest of your records.</li>
    <li><strong>Muscles worked</strong> opens to an anatomy diagram of the primary and secondary muscles.</li>
    <li><strong>Notes &amp; instructions</strong> shows your notes for the exercise and the built-in technique cues. Tap it once it's open to edit your notes. It appears only when there's something to show.</li>
    <li>The <strong>pencil</strong> in the top bar opens the edit screen.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="creating">Create an exercise</h2>
  <p>Tap <strong>+</strong> in the exercise library, or <strong>Add exercise → Create new</strong> during a workout.</p>

  <h3>Required fields</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Name</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Category</strong> - A built-in category or one of your own.</li>
    <li><strong>Exercise type</strong> - Decides which fields you fill in when logging (see below).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Optional fields</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Weight unit</strong> - kg or lbs. Starts on your global setting.</li>
    <li><strong>Default increment</strong> - How much weight the progressive overload suggestion adds.</li>
    <li><strong>Default rest (working sets)</strong> - Rest timer length after a working set.</li>
    <li><strong>Default rest (warmup sets)</strong> - Rest timer length after a warmup set.</li>
    <li><strong>Primary &amp; secondary muscles</strong> - Picked on the body diagram. Used for muscle balance and filtering.</li>
    <li><strong>Plate calculator mode</strong> - Dual-loaded (barbell, leg press) or Single-loaded (T-bar, landmine). Sets how the <a href="/help/tools/">plate calculator</a> splits the weight.</li>
    <li><strong>Bar weight</strong> - Replaces the default bar weight in the plate calculator.</li>
    <li><strong>Compatible machines</strong> - Machines at your gyms that this exercise uses. The training screen offers these when you pick a machine.</li>
    <li><strong>Default graph</strong> - Which progress graph shows first.</li>
    <li><strong>Notes</strong> - Your reminders (form cues, machine settings, links). They show at the top of the training screen when you log this exercise.</li>
    <li><strong>Instructions</strong> - Technique cues, filled in for every built-in exercise. Tap the card on the training screen to view or edit them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="types">Exercise types</h2>
  <p>The type decides which fields you fill in for each set.</p>

  <!-- Styling comes from .prose table: caps head over a 2px ink rule, hairline
       rows. The hand-rolled inline version predated it and fought it. -->
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Type</th>
        <th>Fields logged</th>
        <th>Typical use</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {[
        ['Weight + Reps', 'Weight, reps', 'Most strength exercises'],
        ['Reps Only', 'Reps', 'Bodyweight (push-ups, pull-ups)'],
        ['Time Only', 'Duration', 'Plank, dead hang, cardio machines'],
        ['Weight + Time', 'Weight, duration', 'Farmer carry, weighted plank'],
        ['Distance + Time', 'Distance, duration', 'Running, rowing'],
        ['Weight + Distance', 'Weight, distance', 'Sled push'],
        ['Reps + Time', 'Reps, duration', 'Circuit-style timed sets'],
        ['Reps + Distance', 'Reps, distance', 'Broad jumps'],
        ['Weight Only', 'Weight', 'Static holds'],
        ['Distance Only', 'Distance', 'Walking, cycling distance'],
      ].map(([type, fields, use]) => (
        <tr>
          <td class="type-name">{type}</td>
          <td>{fields}</td>
          <td>{use}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="categories">Categories</h2>
  <p>Every exercise belongs to a category. The 8 built-in ones are Chest, Back, Shoulders, Biceps, Triceps, Legs, Core and Cardio. Categories drive the library's chip row, the exercise picker's filters, and the weekly volume chips on the Today screen.</p>

  <h3>Manage categories</h3>
  <p>In the exercise library, open the <strong>⋮</strong> menu and tap <strong>Manage categories</strong>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tap <strong>New category</strong> to name it and pick a color. A custom category works everywhere a built-in one does, except the muscle heatmap, since it has no muscle group.</li>
    <li>Tap the <strong>pencil icon</strong> to rename a category or change its color, built-in ones included.</li>
    <li>Drag a row's handle to reorder.</li>
    <li>Tap the <strong>trash icon</strong> to delete. Only an empty category can be deleted; if it still holds exercises, Badger tells you how many so you can move or delete them first.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="linking">Linked exercises</h2>
  <p>Link two exercises that are the same movement under different names (<em>Flat Bench Press</em> and <em>Barbell Bench Press</em>, or a free-weight lift and its machine version) and they share progress history and personal records.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the exercise and tap the <strong>pencil</strong>.</li>
    <li>Scroll to <strong>Linked exercises</strong>, below Compatible machines.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>Link exercise</strong> and pick the other exercise.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Both exercises then show the same graphs, PRs, stats and history. A new best on either one counts as a PR on both.</p>
  <p>To unlink, tap the linked exercise's chip (it has an <strong>×</strong>) and confirm. The two stop sharing history, and both stay in the library.</p>
  <p id="merged-duplicates">Looking for <em>Lying Leg Curl</em> or <em>Good Morning (Legs)</em>? They're now <em>Leg Curl (Lying)</em> and <em>Good Morning</em>, and sets you logged under the old names are there, with your records and graphs.</p>

  <h2>Changing exercise settings mid-workout</h2>
  <p>On the training screen, tap the info icon in the top bar (or the notes card, when it's showing) to open the exercise notes screen. Changes there apply to the exercise from now on, in every workout:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Notes and instructions</li>
    <li>Default weight increment</li>
    <li>Default rest times (working and warmup)</li>
    <li>Bar weight for the plate calculator</li>
    <li>Tempo (for example "3-1-2-0")</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The screen also shows the exercise's muscles.</p>

  <h2>Delete an exercise</h2>
  <p>In the exercise library, open the <strong>⋮</strong> menu on the exercise's row and tap <strong>Delete</strong>, then confirm.</p>
  <p>An exercise that's logged in a workout or used in a routine can't be deleted yet. A note shows how many workouts and routines use it; remove it from those first.</p>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  /* The type name is the row's label, so it carries the ink while the two
     explanatory columns stay secondary. */
  .type-name {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
</style>
